On Apr 24, 1:10 pm, syullf....@gmail.com wrote:
> Why do molds become tainted, but not lichens? Molds are fungi, and
> lichens are fungi in a symbiotic relationship with a photosynthesis-
> capable partner, usually algae. I don't see why the presence of algae
> in the lichen would prevent its decay. The only reason I can think of
> that lichens should be immune to decay but not other F would be if
> NetHack's variety of lichens had some kind of antibiotic effect, which
> I would expect to be able to exploit in some way.
>
> Lizard corpses face a similar logic flaw: why don't they decay? It
> seems to be only to allow you to carry one in case of cockatrice
> attacks, which doesn't make a lot of sense.

Both are really for gameplay reasons rather than realism. If you are
keeping a horse as a pet, you will really appreciate the non-decay of
lichens. I suppose that this could be extended to other F, so that it
doesn't stick out like so much of a sore-thumb. What do others think,
would this be unbalancing in any way? Would it make food too
plentiful?

Lizard corpses would be next to useless if they decayed. Your chances
of finding a fresh lizard when you need one is next to nil. Realism
takes a hit in exchange for interesting gameplay.

